source "Kconfig.zephyr"

config ESC_DIAG
	bool "Run link diagnostics instead of the milestone 4a register check"
	default n
	help
	  Diagnostic build, used in place of the milestone 4a register check
	  when the ESC does not answer over SPI.

	  Tests the NSS wire by asserting chip select and watching for the
	  ESC's reaction on IRQ and MISO — NSS and SCK are ESC inputs, so a
	  pull-up cannot tell a carrying wire from a dead one, and this is the
	  only way to see them. Ends in a live monitor of every SPI line so
	  wiring can be poked at without reflashing.

config SERVO_LOCAL_SWEEP
	bool "Sweep the servo locally instead of following EtherCAT"
	default n
	help
	  Milestone 5a behaviour: ignore the bus and sweep the actuator from a
	  local counter. Proves the PWM channel, the actuator wiring and its
	  power supply on their own, so that if the angle later arrives over
	  EtherCAT and nothing moves, the mechanical half is already ruled out.

	  Leave this off for normal operation, where the angle comes from the
	  master as process data.

config ESC_DC_SYNC
	bool "Pace the slave cycle from the SYNC0 distributed-clock output"
	default y
	help
	  Runs one cycle of the slave application per SYNC0 edge instead of
	  polling on a local timer, using the sync0-gpios pin declared on the
	  ESC node in app.overlay (GPIO 21).

	  SYNC0 only pulses after the master calls ec_dcsync0(), which cannot
	  happen before SAFEOP, so the loop polls at 1 ms until edges appear
	  and reverts to polling if they stop. Turning this off costs nothing
	  functionally — the chain works either way — but the actuation instant
	  then follows the master's scheduling jitter instead of the bus clock.

	  This matters little for a hobby servo on a 20 ms PWM frame. It
	  becomes mandatory the moment two axes have to move together.

config ESC_USE_SOES
	bool "Use the SOES slave stack instead of the built-in minimal slave"
	default n
	help
	  Runs OpenEtherCATsociety/SOES (fetched at configure time by
	  firmware/CMakeLists.txt, pinned by commit — not vendored) in place of
	  firmware/src/ecat_slave.c. Both sit on the same ax58100.c
	  SPI layer and present identical process data — 2 bytes out, 6 in — so
	  the master cannot tell them apart.

	  SOES additionally provides a CoE mailbox with SDO access, which the
	  minimal slave has none of, and is the natural foundation for CiA 402.
	  It costs a large external dependency for a node that exchanges four
	  bytes, which is why it is not the default.

	  Configuration lives in firmware/soes/ecat_options.h and must match the
	  module's EEPROM exactly.
